C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not survey the ways the person of Christ has been explained and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes Paul teaches when he writes about who Christ is, it gives the evidence from Paul’s own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own terms."),
("p", "The position is this. Paul says who Christ is in several places, and each saying is shaped by the problem it answers. To a quarrelling church at Philippi he sets one who was “in the form of God” and who took “the form of a servant.” To a church troubled by teachers of philosophy at Colossae he sets one who is “the image of the invisible God,” by whom all things were created, and in whom “dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily.” To a young man charged with the order of a church he writes of the mystery of godliness: “God was manifest in the flesh.” To another he writes of “the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ.” In Romans, among the privileges of Israel, he sets Christ “who is over all, God blessed for ever.” These are not one creed said five times, and none of them uses the words of the later creeds. Read together, without those words, they present one person who is God and who became man. Each passage leaves unsaid how the two are one, and the volume does not say more than the passages say."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each passage is read in its own place: the verses before it, the problem it answers, and what it leaves unsaid. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read in full and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the position is described in the author’s words, and no view is credited to anyone who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume is written for readers who know the passages and want to see them together. It does not require Greek. A few Greek words that carry weight are given in Roman letters in the appendix on key words, with the way the King James Version renders them. The reader is asked to keep a Bible open, to test every row of every table, and to correct the author where the text does."),
("p", "The author does not claim to have said the last word on the person of Christ. The claim is narrower. When Paul’s sayings are read one at a time and then side by side, they say more than a first reading finds, and less than the later words built on them. The volume tries to keep to what they say."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Paul says who Christ is in several passages, each in its own terms and against its own problem. Read together, they present one person who is God and who became man, and each leaves unsaid how the two are one."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "Paul names Christ in several different ways. Philippians 2 sets the form of God before the form of a servant. Colossians 1 and 2 set the image of the invisible God, the Creator, and the fulness of the Godhead dwelling bodily. First Timothy 3 sets God manifest in the flesh. Titus 2 and Romans 9 name him God.", "Chapters 1 to 11"),
    ("2", "Each saying answers a problem of its own: a quarrel (Philippians), rival teachers (Colossians), the order of a church (1 Timothy), the hope of the Body (Titus), the privileges of Israel (Romans 9). None is one creed said five times, and none uses later creedal words.", "Chapters 2, 5, 10, 11, 9"),
    ("3", "Paul also calls him a man, born of a woman and of the seed of David, and the one mediator between God and men. The man and God are named by the same writer, and neither saying cancels the other.", "Chapter 12"),
    ("4", "The passages leave unsaid how the two are one. The volume does not supply the answer, and it does not read later definitions back into Paul.", "Chapters 14 to 16"),
  ], [0.15, 0.57, 0.28]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any one of the passages and ask whether it stands without the others. If it does, the thesis is only partly needed. If each passage is made clearer by the rest, and none by a later creed, the thesis stands."),
]
